\section{The tests}{

All the tests are tests of a composite hypothesis: the increments
\eqn{\Delta x_1, \dots, \Delta x_n} are i.i.d. with a law of the family of
the model, whose parameters are unknown and estimated on the sample.

\strong{Gamma process (\code{"HGP"})}, increments \eqn{\Gamma(a \Delta, b)}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"HME1"}: Henze, Meintanis and Ebner (2012), Laplace transform
(statistic \code{gofgamma::HME1}, tuning parameter 1);
\item \code{"BE"}: Betsch and Ebner (2019), characterization by a fixed point
(statistic \code{gofgamma::BE}, tuning parameter 1);
\item \code{"VGE"}: Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), ratio of two
variance estimators (\code{goft::gamma_test}), with a p-value by
parametric bootstrap;
\item \code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"}, \code{"Watson"}: Kolmogorov-Smirnov,
Cramer-von Mises, Anderson-Darling and Watson (\code{gofgamma}).
}

For the tests HME1, BE, KS, CvM, AD and Watson, the statistic and the
parametric bootstrap are those of the package \code{gofgamma} (the shape is
estimated by \code{gofgamma::gamma_est}, and each bootstrap sample of the
gamma law with this shape is estimated again); all the bootstrap
statistics are kept, which gives both the p-value and the critical value.

\strong{Wiener process (\code{"HWP"})}, increments
\eqn{N(\mu \Delta, \sigma^2 \Delta)}: tests of normality with estimated
mean and variance.
\itemize{
\item \code{"AD"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"Lilliefors"}: Anderson-Darling, Cramer-von Mises and
Lilliefors tests (\code{nortest}). The Lilliefors test is the
Kolmogorov-Smirnov test with estimated mean and variance: \code{"KS"} is also
accepted for it, and the result is labelled \code{"Lilliefors"};
\item \code{"SW"}: Shapiro-Wilk (\code{stats::shapiro.test});
\item \code{"JB"}: Jarque-Bera (\code{tseries::jarque.bera.test});
\item \code{"DAgostinoPearson"}: D'Agostino-Pearson omnibus test \eqn{K^2},
combining skewness and kurtosis (\code{fBasics::dagoTest}).
}

\strong{Inverse Gaussian process (\code{"InvGaus"})}, increments
\eqn{IG(\mu \Delta, \eta \Delta^2)}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"ABEV1"}, \code{"ABEV2"}: Allison, Betsch, Ebner and Visagie (2022)
(\code{gofIG}, tuning parameter 10, maximum likelihood);
\item \code{"HK1"}, \code{"HK2"}: Henze and Klar (2002) (\code{gofIG});
\item \code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"}: Kolmogorov-Smirnov, Cramer-von Mises and
Anderson-Darling (\code{gofIG});
\item \code{"VGE_ratio"}: Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), ratio of two
variance estimators, asymptotic p-value, suited to large samples
(\code{goft::ig_test}, method \code{"ratio"});
\item \code{"VGE_gamma"}: Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), transformation of
the data to gamma variables of shape 1/2 and Anderson-Darling test
(\code{goft::ig_test}, method \code{"transf"}).
}

\strong{Variance-gamma (\code{"VG"}) and Meixner (\code{"Meixner"}) processes}:
\code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"}, \code{"Watson"} (Kolmogorov-Smirnov, Cramer-von Mises,
Anderson-Darling and Watson statistics, Stephens, 1986). The parameters are
estimated by maximum likelihood (\code{\link[levyfit:vg_mle]{levyfit::vg_mle()}},
\code{\link[levyfit:meixner_mle]{levyfit::meixner_mle()}}), the distribution function is computed by
numerical integration of the density, and the p-value is obtained by
parametric bootstrap (Stute, Gonzalez Manteiga and Presedo Quindimil,
1993). With a small sample, the maximum likelihood estimator of the
Meixner law may not exist (maximum on the boundary, towards a shifted
gamma or a Gaussian law): the test is then not computed, with this message.
}

